/-!
# The case of a nilpotent section

Let `A` be an `ℕ`-graded ring and `f ∈ A₁`. If the cohomological dimension of `(f)` is at most
zero, then `V(f)` is open and closed, so it is cut out by an idempotent `e`; then `e ∈ (fⁿ)` for
every `n`, so `e` has no homogeneous components at all, `e = 0`, and `f` is nilpotent.

In the situation of [Stacks, Tag 0EL5], a nilpotent `f` makes the ideal `𝓘` of the zero scheme
of `s` nilpotent (on a chart of consecutive degrees `𝓘` is generated by the chart ratio
`w = f b²/(cb)`, and `wᵏ = 0` once `fᵏ = 0`), and every object of `Coh(X, 𝓘)` is then the
completion of one of its own terms ([Stacks, Tag 0EHP]). This is the case `cd(A, (f)) = 0`,
which [Stacks, Tag 0EJI] (stated for `cd(A, I) = 1`) does not cover; `cd(A, (f)) ≤ 1` always,
`(f)` being principal.

## Main results

* `Stacks.LefschetzCone.isNilpotent_of_cohomologicalDimension_le_zero`
* `Stacks.LefschetzCone.isCompletionOfCoherent_of_isNilpotent`

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0EL5](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0EL5)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0EHP](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0EHP)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0EJI](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0EJI)
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

open AlgebraicGeometry CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits Opposite TopologicalSpace
open Stacks.GradedSections (powSections mul sectionRing)
open Stacks.AmpleFiniteAvoidance (nvOpen grading)
open Stacks.SectionRingProjMorphism (projHomOfIsAmple)
